==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|Napoleon}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|Napoléon||given name, usually in reference to {{w|Napoleon Bonaparte}}}}. In reference to coins, via {{bor|en|fr|napoléon||(slang) a coin that originally had the appearance of Napoleon's face in the obverse}}.<ref>"[http://www.oed.com/view/Entry/258537 Napoleon, ''n.¹'']" in the ''Oxford English Dictionary'' (2003), Oxford: Oxford University Press.</ref> In reference to guns and possibly cards, in reference to {{w|Napoleon III}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/nəˈpəʊlɪən/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/nəˈpoʊli.ən/|/nəˈpoʊljn/|/nəˈpoʊljən/}}
*: {{rhymes|en|əʊliən|s=4}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|historical|_|numismatics|slang}} The [[franc germinal]]: a 20-[[franc]] [[gold coin]] [[issue]]d under [[w:Napoleon I of France|Napoleon]].
# {{lb|en|historical|_|numismatics|slang}} [[other|Other]] [[subsequent]] 20-[[franc]] [[coin]]s, [[note]]s, or [[value]]s.
#* '''1891''', {{w|Arthur Conan Doyle}}, ''{{w|The Adventure of the Red-Headed League}}'' (Norton 2005 p.67)
#*: We had occasion some months ago to strengthen our resources, and borrowed, for that purpose, thirty thousand '''napoleons''' from the Bank of France.
# {{lb|en|usually|_|admiringly|_|pejorative}} A [[person]] [[resembling]] [[w:Napoleon Bonaparte|Napoleon Bonaparte]], {{label|en|usually}} in [[having]] [[come]] to [[dominate]] an [[area]] or [[sphere]] of [[activity]] through [[ruthlessness]] or [[illegality]] [[as well as]] [[surpassing]] [[ability]].
#* '''1894''', [[w:Arthur Conan Doyle|Arthur Ignatius Conan Doyle]], ''The Memoirs of Sherlock Holmes'', p. 260:
#*: He [''[[sc.]]'' [[w:James Moriarty|James Moriarty]]] is the [[napoleon|Napoleon]] of crime, [[w:John Watson|Watson]]. He is the organizer of half that is evil and of nearly all that is undetected in this great city [''[[sc.]]'' [[London]]]. He is a genius, a philosopher, an abstract thinker.
#* '''1896''', J.S. Ogilvie, ''The Life and Speeches of William McKinley:''
#*: He [''[[sc.]]'' [[w:William McKinley|McKinley]]] has been called the '''Napoleon''' of protection, as other men have been called '''Napoleons''' of finance.
